/** @page user-guide User guide

@section getting-started Getting started

Currently there are no pre-built binaries, so you will need to build from
source.

Make sure you have the following prerequisites:
- CMake >= 3.20
- Python 3
- C++20 compiler

Then:

@code{.sh}
git clone https://github.com/cpp-hardtech/rtl-wiring-inspector.git
cd rtl-wiring-inspector
cmake -B build \
    -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
    -DENABLE_PY_BINDINGS=ON \
    -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=$PWD/install
cmake --build build -j --target install
ctest --test-dir build
@endcode

When using the Python bindings, it is recommended to use the pyslang shared
object file that is produced as part of the build. Different versions of
the upstream slang Python bindings may not work.


@section cli-usage CLI usage

There are two command-line tools available. @c rtl-wiring-inspector exposes the
netlist-graph building and querying functions of the library and answers
structural questions about a design (fan-in/fan-out, path queries,
combinational loops, register inventory). @c rtl-report is its companion
tool: while you are exploring a design with @c rtl-wiring-inspector it surfaces the
underlying AST-level information that the netlist abstracts away: port
declarations, variable / net declarations with type and width, the full
driver set for each value symbol, and the elaborated AST itself as JSON.
The two tools share the same source-elaboration front end, so a question
that turns out to be about source-level data rather than connectivity can be
answered without re-compiling. Both tools accept SystemVerilog source files
and the standard slang compilation options. Run either with @c --help for
the full option list.

@subsection cli-general General options (rtl-wiring-inspector)

- @c -h, @c --help: display available options.
- @c --version: print version information and exit.
- @c --no-colours: disable coloured output.
- @c --colour: force coloured output even when stdout is not a terminal.
  Colours are on by default only when stdout is an interactive terminal;
  @c --no-colours takes precedence over @c --colour.
- @c -q, @c --quiet: suppress non-essential output.
- @c -d, @c --debug: enable debug logging (debug builds only).
- @c -j, @c --threads @c \<N\>: set the number of threads (0 = hardware
  concurrency, 1 = sequential).
- @c --stats: print phase timings and peak memory to stderr.
- @c --stats-json: print phase timings and peak memory in JSON format to
  stderr. Statistics never mix with query results on stdout, so the two
  streams can be parsed independently. Either option on its own is a
  complete action: the netlist is built and its statistics printed.
- @c --no-resolve-assign-bits: disable bit-aligned dependency resolution of
  concatenations, replications, conversions, and equal-width conditional
  operators in assignments and port connections; see
  @ref bit-dependency-resolution.
- @c --no-prop-cuts-across-ports: disable propagation of concat-induced
  cut points across module port boundaries. With this set, port nodes
  and module-internal assignments stay whole-word at port boundaries
  and paths through concatenated ports become bit-imprecise; see
  @ref bit-dependency-resolution.
- @c --expand-operations: expand binary, unary and conditional operators
  on the right-hand side of an assignment into @c Operation nodes, so that
  a traced path names each operator it passes through instead of reporting
  a single anonymous assignment. Off by default; see
  @ref bit-dependency-resolution for what it does and does not cover.
- @c --black-box @c \<pattern\>: treat instances whose definition name or
  hierarchical path matches the glob pattern as black boxes (see
  @ref glob-syntax for the full pattern syntax). Black-boxed instances
  get port nodes and external wiring, but their body is not traversed,
  so paths terminate at the boundary. The option may be repeated to
  supply multiple patterns.

@subsection cli-queries Query commands

@c --from @c \<name\> @c --to @c \<name\>: find a path between two named
points. Names are hierarchical paths such as @c top.sub.signal. The path is
printed with source locations when available.

@code{.ansi}
rtl-wiring-inspector design.sv --from top.a --to top.b
@endcode

A path query prints an alternating sequence of nodes and edges from the start
signal to the end signal. Each step shows a source location and a description:

- @b "input port" / @b "output port": a module port boundary.
- @b "assignment": a continuous or procedural assignment.
- @b "conditional statement" / @b "case statement": a branch in a procedural
  block.
- @b "value": the edge between two nodes, annotated with the driven signal
  name and bit range.

When the design has been compiled with the slang AST available, source locations
are available and the output includes annotated source lines; otherwise after
loading a serialised netlist, a plain @c file:line:col format is used.

Pass @c --format @c json to render a path as machine-readable JSON, or
@c -o @c \<file\> to write the report to a file instead of stdout. The JSON
form is an array of alternating steps:

- a node step: @c {\"type\":\"node\", \"node_kind\": ..., \"name\": ...,
  \"path\": ..., \"location\": ...}, where @c node_kind is the node kind
  (@c Port, @c State, @c Assignment, ...), @c name is the symbol name when the
  node has one, @c path is its hierarchical path, and @c location is
  @c file:line:col;
- an edge step: @c {\"type\":\"edge\", \"symbol_path\": ...,
  \"bits\": ..., \"edge_kind\": ...}.

The array starts and ends with a node step and contains exactly one fewer edge
step than node steps.

A single name can resolve to several split nodes, so the search tries every
combination of same-named start and end nodes and reports the first path found.

Either endpoint may be given on its own. @c --from @c \<name\> alone reports the
combinational fan-out cone from the node (equivalent to @c --fan-out), and
@c --to @c \<name\> alone reports the combinational fan-in cone to it
(equivalent to @c --fan-in). An explicit @c --fan-out / @c --fan-in takes
precedence over the aliased endpoint.

@c --fan-out @c \<name\>: report the combinational fan-out cone from a named
node (all nodes reachable via combinational edges, stopping at registers).

@c --fan-in @c \<name\>: report the combinational fan-in cone to a named
node.

@c --sensitivity @c \<name\>: report the clocks and resets gating a named
node. For a register the node's own clocking edges are listed; for a
combinational node the union of the clocking edges over every register in
its combinational fan-out. When a name resolves to both a register State node
and a same-named port, the register's own edges are used, so the clocks of
the logic it feeds do not leak into the answer. Each entry shows the source
signal, its edge kind, and source location.

@c --constant-drivers @c \<name\>: report the constant values driving a
named signal when every bit slice is tied off to literals. A register or
external input reaching any slice leaves the report empty. Each entry
shows the constant value and source location.

@c --drivers @c \<name[bit-range]\>: report, per bit, which node drives a
named signal. Each row shows a contiguous bit range, the driving node
(assignment, register, port, constant, ...) and its source location. An
optional bit-range suffix narrows and clips the query, e.g.
@c m.sig[3:0] or @c m.sig[2]; without one the whole signal is reported.
This exposes the graph's bit-level connectivity directly.

Only write edges count as drivers, so reading a signal does not make it look
self-driven. A name can be a declared signal, a register, an output port, or an
internal wire/reg that has no node of its own: internal signals are resolved
from the symbols annotated on their driving edges. A hierarchical input port
reports the actual wired to it. A name that is present in the design but has
no known driver produces an empty table; only a name absent from the design
is an error.

@c --find @c \<pattern\>: find named nodes matching a glob pattern.
A signal split across several nodes is listed once. See @ref glob-syntax
for the full pattern syntax.

@c --find-regex @c \<pattern\>: find named nodes matching a regex pattern.

@par Filtering by scope or name

The node-listing query commands (@c --report-registers, @c --find,
@c --find-regex, @c --fan-out, @c --fan-in, and @c --sensitivity) accept
@c --scope @c \<path\> and @c --name @c \<pattern\> to constrain their output.
@c --scope keeps only nodes within a hierarchical subtree: a node passes if its
path is the scope or a descendant of it, so @c top.cpu matches @c top.cpu.alu.x
but not @c top.cpu2. An array scope such as @c top.u_arr includes all of its
indexed elements. Use an indexed path such as @c top.u_arr[0] to select one.
@c --name keeps only nodes whose hierarchical path matches
a glob pattern (see @ref glob-syntax). Both options may be repeated; a node
passes if it lies in one of the scopes @b and matches one of the names. Scopes
are literal paths. Use @c --name for glob matching across a subtree.

@code{.ansi}
rtl-wiring-inspector design.sv --report-registers --scope top.cpu --name "**.q_reg"
@endcode

@par Output format

The tabular query and report commands (@c --report-registers, @c --find,
@c --find-regex, @c --fan-out, @c --fan-in, @c --sensitivity,
@c --constant-drivers, and @c --drivers), plus the @c --from/@c --to path
query and @c --comb-loops, accept @c --format @c \<table|json\>.
For a path, @c --format @c json emits the alternating node/edge step array
described above; for combinational loops it emits the loop array. @c table is
the default and leaves each command's native text output unchanged.
The default @c table renders an aligned text table; @c json emits an array of
objects keyed by the lower-cased column headers (@c name, @c location,
@c edge, @c value, @c bits, @c driver), which is convenient for scripting and
CI. Pass @c -o / @c --output
@c \<file\> to write the output to a file instead of stdout (@c - for stdout).

@code{.ansi}
rtl-wiring-inspector design.sv --report-registers --format json -o registers.json
@endcode

@subsection cli-reports Reports

@c --report-registers: list all registers (@c State nodes) in the design.
A State node is created for any variable that is assigned inside a clocked
procedural block (@c always_ff or an @c always block with an edge-sensitive
event list). Each entry shows the hierarchical path and source location. A
register split into several State nodes by bit-aligned resolution is listed
once.

@c --comb-loops: detect and report combinational loops. Reports cycles in
the graph that consist entirely of combinational (non-clocked) edges. Each
loop is printed as a path from a node back to itself. A reported loop means
the tool found a structural cycle in the source-level dependency graph; see
@ref bit-dependency-resolution for caveats on precision.

The @c --scope and @c --name filters apply to loop reports: a loop is kept
when at least one of its nodes passes the filters. Pass @c --format @c json
to emit loops as JSON instead: an array of objects with @c length (the number
of nodes in the cycle), a @c nodes array of node steps, and an @c edges array
of edge steps. The edges include the closing edge, so both arrays have
@c length entries. An empty array is printed when no loop is found. The node
and edge step schemas are the same as for path JSON.

For AST-level reports (variables, ports, drivers, AST JSON dump) use
@c rtl-report, see @ref cli-report below.

@subsection cli-export Export and serialization

@c --netlist-dot @c \<file\>: write the netlist graph in DOT format.
Use @c - for stdout. The output contains one graph node per @c NetlistNode
and one graph edge per @c NetlistEdge. Node labels show the node kind and
hierarchical path where applicable; edge labels show the driven signal and
bit range. The DOT output can be rendered with Graphviz tools such as
@c dot, @c neato, or @c fdp:

@code{.ansi}
rtl-wiring-inspector design.sv --netlist-dot - | dot -Tsvg -o netlist.svg
@endcode

By default the whole graph is rendered. Combine @c --netlist-dot with a
@c --fan-out, @c --fan-in, or @c --from/@c --to selector to render only the
relevant cone or path, the induced subgraph over those nodes, instead of
the entire design:

@code{.ansi}
rtl-wiring-inspector design.sv --netlist-dot - --fan-out top.sub.sig | dot -Tsvg -o cone.svg
@endcode

@c --save-netlist @c \<file\>: serialize the netlist graph to a JSON file
for later reloading. Saving takes precedence over the query and report
options, which are not run when it is given.

@c --load-netlist @c \<file\>: load a previously saved netlist (skips
compilation). Analysis commands such as @c --from/@c --to, @c --comb-loops,
and @c --report-registers work on loaded netlists, as does
@c --save-netlist, which writes the loaded graph back out.

@subsection cli-report rtl-report

@c rtl-report is the companion tool to @c rtl-wiring-inspector: it surfaces
AST-level information so that while you are exploring a design with
@c rtl-wiring-inspector you can pivot to a question about the underlying source,
such as "what type is this variable", "how wide is this port" or "who
drives this signal", without re-compiling. It runs reports against the
elaborated AST and accepts the standard slang compilation options plus
exactly one of the following actions:

- @c --ports: list every port (direction, hierarchical path, width, net
  type, source location). The net type is the underlying nettype name
  (@c wire, @c wand, ...) for net ports and @c var for variable ports.
  A concatenated non-ANSI port has one entry with its total width and
  effective direction. Its net type is @c mixed when its members use
  different net types or combine nets with variables.
- @c --variables: list every variable and net declaration (name, declared
  type, width, kind, driver count, source location). Kind is @c var for
  variable symbols and the nettype name for nets. The driver count is
  taken from slang's analysis pass, so this action triggers analysis.
- @c --drivers: for every value symbol, list each known driver with its
  bit range, driving expression, kind (@c proc for procedural,
  @c cont for continuous), and source location. Triggers analysis.
- @c --ast-json @c \<file\>: dump the compiled AST as JSON to the given
  file (@c - for stdout). Table output is not meaningful for this mode.

Port and variable widths are the total bit counts of fixed-size types,
including unpacked arrays and structs. Types without a statically known
size report a width of @c 0.

Passing more than one action is an error. A report switch set to @c false
leaves that action disabled.

@par Output format
Use @c --format @c table (the default) or @c --format @c json on the
three tabular actions to choose human-readable columns or
machine-readable JSON. @c --ast-json is always JSON. The JSON drivers
report nests the per-value drivers under each value entry. An unknown
@c --format value is rejected regardless of the selected action; a valid
value is ignored by @c --ast-json.

@par Output destination
By default the tabular actions print to stdout; pass @c -o /
@c --output @c \<file\> to redirect to a file (use @c - for stdout).
@c --ast-json takes its file path directly on the flag.

@par Filtering
All four actions accept @c --scope @c \<path\> to restrict reporting to
one or more named hierarchical scopes. @c \<path\> may be a literal
hierarchical name (resolved via slang's name lookup) or a glob pattern
expanded by walking the elaborated tree. @c rtl-report errors if a
literal scope does not resolve or if a glob matches nothing.

Scopes are applied to every instantiated scope in the design, so repeated
instances of the same module report their own hierarchical paths. Overlap
is removed before reporting: a symbol selected by more than one scope, or
by both an ancestor and one of its descendants, is listed once. A glob
that matches a scope covers that scope's whole subtree.

Port, variable, and driver reports omit declarations in inactive generate
branches of an elaborated design.

The three tabular actions additionally accept @c --name @c \<pattern\>
to restrict by hierarchical path. Multiple @c --name patterns combine
with OR semantics; an empty match returns an empty result rather than
an error (the flag is a filter, not a selector).

Both @c --scope and @c --name use the glob syntax described in
@ref glob-syntax (@c *, @c ?, @c **, @c ...).

Use @c --direction with @c --ports to report only @c input, @c output,
@c inout, or @c ref ports. Repeat it to include multiple directions.
It combines with @c --scope and @c --name in both table and JSON output.

@par AST JSON shape
Without @c --scope, @c --ast-json dumps the root symbol. When exactly
one scope resolves, it dumps a single JSON object for that scope. When
several scopes resolve, it emits a JSON array with one object per scope,
so the output is always one valid JSON value.

@code{.ansi}
# Inspect a single instance's output ports.
rtl-report design.sv --ports --scope top.cpu --direction output

# All variables in the design whose name ends in '_q', as JSON.
rtl-report design.sv --variables --name '**.*_q' --format json

# Drivers of a single net, written to a file.
rtl-report design.sv --drivers --name top.bus.cmd -o cmd-drivers.txt

# AST dump of just one scope.
rtl-report design.sv --ast-json - --scope top.cpu
@endcode


@section python-bindings Python bindings

The @c rtl_wiring_inspector module (enabled with @c -DENABLE_PY_BINDINGS=ON)
exposes the graph data structures and analysis routines to Python. It
depends on @c pyslang for the compilation and analysis types.

@subsection python-building Building a graph

The build pipeline has four steps: force lazy AST construction with
@c VisitAll, freeze the compilation, run @c AnalysisManager, then unfreeze
the compilation so @c NetlistGraph.build can keep elaborating.

@code{.py}
import pyslang
import rtl_wiring_inspector

# Compile.
tree = pyslang.syntax.SyntaxTree.fromText("""
    module m(input logic a, output logic b);
        assign b = a;
    endmodule
""")
compilation = pyslang.ast.Compilation()
compilation.addSyntaxTree(tree)

# Elaborate the design, which analysis requires. Check for errors here.
assert not any(d.isError() for d in compilation.getAllDiagnostics())

# Force construction of the whole AST before freezing, since AST
# construction is lazy and visiting a previously-unvisited node can
# mutate the compilation (which is not thread-safe).
rtl_wiring_inspector.VisitAll().run(compilation)
compilation.freeze()

# Run analysis.
am = pyslang.analysis.AnalysisManager()
am.analyze(compilation)

# Unfreeze before building: the netlist builder needs to keep
# elaborating the AST.
compilation.unfreeze()

# Build the netlist.
graph = rtl_wiring_inspector.NetlistGraph()
graph.build(compilation, am)
@endcode

@c NetlistGraph.build accepts these keyword arguments:

- @c parallel (default @c True): dispatch DFA work and R-value resolution
  across a thread pool.
- @c num_threads (default @c 0): thread pool size; @c 0 uses hardware
  concurrency.
- @c resolve_assign_bits (default @c True): bit-aligned dependency
  resolution; see @ref bit-dependency-resolution.
- @c prop_cuts_across_ports (default @c True): propagate concat-induced
  cut points across port boundaries; see @ref bit-dependency-resolution.
- @c black_boxes (default @c []): list of glob patterns matched against
  each instance's definition name and hierarchical path (see
  @ref glob-syntax). Matched instances skip body traversal and record
  only port-boundary connectivity.

@subsection python-querying Querying the graph

@code{.py}
# Graph size.
print(graph.num_nodes(), graph.num_edges())

# Lookup a node by hierarchical name.
node = graph.lookup("m.a") # Returns NetlistNode or None

# Lookup nodes by name and bit range overlap.
nodes = graph.lookup_by_range("m.a", 0, 7)

# Find nodes by wildcard or regex.
nodes = graph.find_nodes("m.*")
nodes = graph.find_nodes_regex(r"m\.[ab]")

# Get drivers of a signal over a bit range.
drivers = graph.get_drivers("m.b", 0, 0)

# Get all drivers over the full bit range of any node, works with node types that
# do not have an inherent bit range (e.g. Assignment nodes).
drivers = graph.get_drivers(node)

# Combinational fan-out / fan-in (stops at State nodes).
fan_out = graph.get_comb_fan_out(node)
fan_in  = graph.get_comb_fan_in(node)

# Per-bit drivers keep one entry per contributing edge, clipped to the
# sub-range of the queried symbol that the driver covers.
for bit_driver in graph.get_bit_drivers("m.y"):
    lower, upper = bit_driver.bounds
    print(lower, upper, bit_driver.driver.kind)

# Restrict the query to a bit range.
bit_drivers = graph.get_bit_drivers("m.y", 3, 5)

# Traverse the edges attached to a node. Each edge exposes its source and
# target nodes, the driven symbol, and the clock sensitivity.
for edge in node.out_edges:
    print(edge.source.ID, edge.target.ID, edge.edge_kind, edge.symbol_path)
for edge in node.in_edges:
    print(edge.source.ID, edge.target.ID, edge.bounds, edge.disabled)
@endcode

@subsection python-serialization Saving and loading netlists

@c NetlistSerializer reads and writes the same versioned JSON format as the
@c --save-netlist and @c --load-netlist CLI options, so a netlist saved by
either side can be loaded by the other.

@code{.py}
# Serialise to the versioned JSON format.
text = rtl_wiring_inspector.NetlistSerializer.serialize(graph)

# Restore it into a new, empty graph.
loaded = rtl_wiring_inspector.NetlistGraph()
rtl_wiring_inspector.NetlistSerializer.deserialize(text, loaded)
@endcode

The destination graph must be empty. Malformed JSON, a missing field, and an
unsupported format version raise @c RuntimeError. The @c blackBoxes field is
round-tripped too, so @ref python-black-boxes coverage queries work on a
loaded graph.

@subsection python-black-boxes Black-box coverage

@c NetlistGraph.build accepts a @c black_boxes list as described in
@ref black-boxes. The graph records the resolved hierarchical path of each
black-boxed instance and classifies nodes against those paths:

@code{.py}
graph.build(compilation, am, black_boxes=["ram_*", "top.cpu.fpu"])

print(graph.get_black_box_paths())

coverage = graph.get_black_box_coverage(graph.lookup("top.cpu.fpu.rdata"))
if coverage == rtl_wiring_inspector.BlackBoxCoverage.Boundary:
    print("port on the black-box boundary")
elif coverage == rtl_wiring_inspector.BlackBoxCoverage.Contained:
    print("inside the black box")
else:
    print("outside every black box")
@endcode

@c BlackBoxCoverage.Outside covers nodes outside every box (including nodes
without a hierarchical path), @c Boundary a port directly below a black box,
and @c Contained anything else at or below one.

@subsection python-pathfinding Path finding

@code{.py}
finder = rtl_wiring_inspector.PathFinder()

# Find any path.
path = finder.find(start_node, end_node)

# Find a combinational-only path (skips State nodes).
path = finder.find_comb(start_node, end_node)

if not path.empty():
    for node in path:
        print(node.kind, getattr(node, "path", ""))
@endcode

@c path.front(), @c path.back() and indexing with @c path[i] raise
@c IndexError when the path is empty or the index is out of range.

@subsection python-iterating Iterating over nodes

@code{.py}
# Iterate all nodes.
for node in graph:
    print(node.ID, node.kind)

# Access typed properties on specific node kinds.
if node.kind == rtl_wiring_inspector.NodeKind.Port:
    port = node  # Port subclass
    print(port.name, port.path, port.bounds, port.is_input())
@endcode

Available node types and their properties:
- @c Port: @c name, @c path, @c direction, @c bounds, @c is_input(),
  @c is_output(), @c is_driven()
- @c Variable: @c name, @c path, @c bounds
- @c State: @c name, @c path, @c bounds
- @c Constant: @c width, @c value (string form of the constant value).
  Created for literal RHSs, constant-foldable expressions, zero-extension
  padding bits, literal port connections, and constant arms of
  conditional operators.
- @c Assignment, @c Conditional, @c Case, @c Merge: no additional properties

The @c bounds property on nodes and edges is a @c DriverBitRange with
@c lower, @c upper, and @c width accessors; it also unpacks as a
@c (lower, @c upper) tuple. A @c BitDriver from
@c NetlistGraph.get_bit_drivers has the same @c bounds property plus a
@c driver node reference.

Every node exposes @c out_edges and @c in_edges as lists of @c NetlistEdge
references. Edge properties: @c source, @c target, @c edge_kind (a
@c pyslang.ast.EdgeKind clock sensitivity), @c symbol_name, @c symbol_path,
@c bounds, @c disabled. Edge and node references are views into the graph, so
keep the owning @c NetlistGraph alive while using them.


@section performance Performance tuning

@subsection perf-threads Threading

Use @c -j @c \<N\> to set the number of threads. The default (@c -j @c 0)
uses hardware concurrency. For small designs the overhead of thread
management can outweigh the benefit; @c -j @c 1 forces sequential execution.

The parallel phases of construction are Phase 2 (DFA dispatch) and Phase 4
(R-value resolution). Phase 2 dispatches one task per procedural block and
continuous assignment, so designs with many independent blocks benefit most
from additional threads. Phase 4 only parallelises when the number of pending
R-values exceeds an internal threshold (1000 by default).

@subsection perf-stats Profiling

Use @c --stats to print a human-readable timing breakdown to stderr:

@code{.ansi}
rtl-wiring-inspector design.sv --report-registers --stats
@endcode

The output shows per-phase timings (parsing, elaboration, analysis, netlist
construction), per-task statistics (min/max/mean/median), the netlist size,
and peak RSS. The Netlist Build section is printed for every newly built
graph, including designs that defer no blocks.

Use @c --stats-json for machine-readable JSON output, also on stderr. Its
@c time_seconds object holds one entry per phase, @c graph_source is
@c \"built\" or @c \"loaded\", and @c black_boxes lists the requested
patterns, the matched instance paths, and the patterns that matched nothing.
When a netlist is loaded with @c --load-netlist, the load time is reported as
@c time_seconds.load, @c graph_source is @c \"loaded\", and
@c netlist_profile is omitted because no construction took place.

@subsection perf-considerations Considerations

There are various factors that affect performance and how much multithreading
improves this. Here are some to consider; note that the execution phases of the
netlist construction are detailed in @ref arch-construction.

- @b Design @b size. More ports, variables and instances means more nodes
  and edges to create in an sequential traversal of the AST (Phase 1).
- @b Procedural @b block @b count. Each @c always / @c initial block is a
  separate DFA task in Phase 2; designs with many small blocks parallelise
  well, while designs dominated by a few large blocks may be limited by the
  longest task.
- @b R-value @b count. Complex expressions with many operands generate more
  pending R-values for Phase 4 to resolve.
- @b Hierarchy @b depth: deeply nested module instantiations increase the
  AST traversal time and the number of port connections to process.


@section analysis-model Analysis model

RTL Wiring Inspector builds its dependency graph by analysing the SystemVerilog source
directly, without performing logic synthesis. This makes it fast, portable
across target technologies, and able to operate on partial or non-synthesisable
designs. However, because the tool does not flatten the design down to a gate-
or LUT-level netlist, the analysis is necessarily more conservative than what a
synthesis tool could provide. The key differences are described below.

@subsection analysis-no-synthesis No-synthesis elaboration

A synthesis tool resolves every operator, generate construct, and hierarchical
reference into a flat network of primitive gates or technology cells. Slang
Netlist instead works at the SystemVerilog source level: it preserves the
original module hierarchy, treats each assignment as an atomic operation and
records data dependencies between the variables that appear on each side. This
means the graph is a faithful representation of the source structure, but it
does not always capture the Boolean-level logic that a synthesised netlist would
expose.

@subsection bit-dependency-resolution Bit dependency resolution

When an assignment connects symbols on its left- and right-hand sides, the
graph records dependencies bit-by-bit wherever the source structure makes
the per-bit correspondence clear. Concatenations, replications, and
width-changing conversions are tracked precisely, so the bits of one symbol
are connected only to the bits they actually drive on the other side:

@code{.sv}
assign {a, b} = {x, y};   // x connects to a, y connects to b
assign w = {2{p}};        // p connects to both halves of w
assign z[31:0] = q[15:0]; // only the lower 16 bits of z have a driver;
                          // the upper 16 are zero-extended
@endcode

This holds across the structural rearrangements you can write in
SystemVerilog: nesting concatenations, replicating with a constant count,
widening to a larger type (the extra bits are recorded as zero- or
sign-extension and have no driver), and using a conditional operator
@c \?: where both arms have the same width as the result (the condition
itself becomes an extra dependency on every bit).

The same rules apply to port connections, which are treated as assignments.
A hookup such as <tt>.in({x,y})</tt> on a two-bit input port connects
@c x and @c y to the matching individual bits of the port.

Bit precision also carries through the port itself. When a concat appears
on a port connection, the cut points are propagated inward so that the
formal port nodes and the module-internal assignments are split at the
same bit boundaries. For example, given:

@code{.sv}
module sub(input logic [1:0] x, output logic [1:0] y);
  assign y = x;
endmodule

module top(input logic a, b, output logic c, d);
  sub u (.x({b, a}), .y({d, c}));
endmodule
@endcode

The graph reports @c a→c and @c b→d but no cross-bit paths.

@par Known limitations

- Operators are not analysed bit-by-bit. As soon as an expression contains
  arithmetic (@c +, @c -, @c *, …), a bitwise or relational operator, a
  reduction, a function call, a streaming concatenation, a non-constant
  select, a narrowing conversion, or a pattern-bearing conditional, every
  symbol read inside that expression is treated as contributing to every
  bit the expression produces. So in <tt>y = a & b</tt> the graph still
  records that every bit of @c y depends on every bit of @c a and @c b,
  even though @c & is bitwise in nature.
- Bit-aligned resolution only applies to integral types whose widths agree
  on both sides. Strings, unpacked aggregates, and dynamically-sized types,
  along with assignments where the two sides have different selectable
  widths (for example an enum coerced to a logic at a port boundary), fall
  back to a coarser per-symbol connection.

@par Fallback options

Bit-aligned resolution can be turned off with @c --no-resolve-assign-bits on the
@c rtl-wiring-inspector CLI, or by passing @c resolve_assign_bits=False to @c
NetlistGraph.build in the Python bindings. With it off, every symbol on one side
of an assignment is connected to every symbol on the other side, matching the
behaviour of releases before bit-aligned resolution landed.

Cross-port cut propagation can be turned off with @c --no-prop-cuts-across-ports
on the @c rtl-wiring-inspector CLI or by passing @c prop_cuts_across_ports=False to @c
NetlistGraph.build in the Python bindings.  With it off, port nodes and
module-internal assignments stay whole-word at port boundaries even when the
actual side of the connection is a concat.

Operator expansion is off by default and is turned on with
@c --expand-operations on the @c rtl-wiring-inspector CLI, or by passing
@c expand_operations=True to @c NetlistGraph.build in the Python bindings.
It adds nodes rather than dependencies: the paths that exist with it off
also exist with it on, each now naming the operators it passes through. It
never removes one, though it can expose a dependency the default path
misses when a conditional arm assigns. It does not make
<tt>y = a & b</tt> bit-precise, as the bit-level fan-in is unchanged and
the limitation above still describes it.

Expansion never reaches a top-level @c \?: in an assignment, because slang
binds the narrow arm of <tt>y = c ? a : b</tt> as a width-extending
conversion, so bit-aligned resolution decomposes it into slices before an
@c Operation node could be created for it. Only a @c \?: nested under
another operator, such as in <tt>y = (c ? a : b) & mask</tt>, is expanded.
Operators nested inside a conditional arm, or inside the right operand of
@c && or @c ||, stay opaque, since those operands are traversed by the
flow analysis so that the definitions reaching each branch are rejoined
correctly.

In every fallback case the resulting graph is still sound (no real
connection is missed) but path and dependency queries over the affected
symbols may report paths that a deeper Boolean analysis could rule out.

@subsection black-boxes Black-boxed instances

Instances can be black-boxed to stop the netlist builder from descending
into their bodies. A black-boxed instance still receives port nodes and
external port-connection wiring, but its internal assignments and
sub-instances are not traversed, so fan-in/fan-out paths terminate at the
port boundary. This is useful for excluding vendor IP, technology cells,
or any sub-tree whose internals are irrelevant to the analysis at hand.

Patterns are glob expressions (see @ref glob-syntax) and are matched
against either an instance's definition name or its hierarchical
instance path, so the same pattern can target a single instance or
every instantiation of a module:

@code{.ansi}
rtl-wiring-inspector design.sv --black-box ram_* --black-box top.cpu.fpu
@endcode

A pattern that matches no instance is reported as a warning on stderr and
otherwise ignored; it does not change the netlist or the exit status. Both
@c --stats and @c --stats-json report the requested patterns, the matched
instance paths, and any unmatched patterns.

The Python equivalent passes a list of patterns to @c NetlistGraph.build:

@code{.py}
graph.build(compilation, am, black_boxes=["ram_*", "top.cpu.fpu"])
@endcode

The resolved paths and per-node classification are available from Python via
@c NetlistGraph.get_black_box_paths and @c NetlistGraph.get_black_box_coverage;
see @ref python-black-boxes.

@subsection glob-syntax Glob pattern syntax

Glob patterns are used to match hierarchical names by the
@c --black-box and @c --find CLI flags, by the @c black_boxes argument
to @c NetlistGraph.build, and by @c NetlistGraph::findNodes. Names are
treated as dot-separated path segments (for example @c top.sub.signal
is three segments).

The supported wildcards are:

<table>
<tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Description</th></tr>
<tr><td>@c *</td>
    <td>Matches zero or more characters within a single path segment.
        Does not cross @c .</td></tr>
<tr><td>@c ** or @c ...</td>
    <td>Matches zero or more characters across path boundaries
        (recursive). @c ... is the LRM-native spelling used elsewhere
        in slang; @c ** is also accepted for consistency with
        widespread tooling convention.</td></tr>
<tr><td>@c ?</td>
    <td>Matches exactly one character within a single path segment.
        Does not match @c .</td></tr>
</table>

Any other character is matched literally.

When a recursive wildcard is written adjacent to a literal @c . in the
pattern (forms such as @c a.**.b, @c a.**, or @c **.b), the adjacent
@c . is treated as an optional segment boundary. This mirrors
gitignore-style recursive globbing and slang's LRM-style @c .../
handling and lets the recursive wildcard stand in for zero or more
intermediate path segments.

Examples:

<table>
<tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Matches</th><th>Does not match</th></tr>
<tr><td>@c top.*</td>
    <td>@c top.a, @c top.foo</td>
    <td>@c top.a.b (crosses @c .), @c top (no segment)</td></tr>
<tr><td>@c top.**</td>
    <td>@c top, @c top.a, @c top.a.b.c</td>
    <td>@c topX</td></tr>
<tr><td>@c top....leaf</td>
    <td>@c top.leaf, @c top.mid.leaf, @c top.a.b.leaf</td>
    <td>@c topXleaf</td></tr>
<tr><td>@c top.u_?</td>
    <td>@c top.u_a, @c top.u_b</td>
    <td>@c top.u_ab, @c top.u_a.x</td></tr>
<tr><td>@c **.leaf</td>
    <td>@c leaf, @c mid.leaf, @c top.mid.leaf</td>
    <td>@c xleaf</td></tr>
</table>

@subsection sv-support SystemVerilog support

The following SystemVerilog constructs are handled during netlist construction:

- Module and interface hierarchies, including generate blocks and arrays of
  instances.
- Input, output, and inout ports; port connections (named and positional).
- Interface modport ports and their connection expressions.
- Continuous assignments (@c assign).
- Procedural blocks: @c always, @c always_ff, @c always_comb, @c initial.
- Procedural assignments (blocking @c = and non-blocking @c <=).
- Conditional statements (@c if/@c else) and @c case statements within
  procedural blocks, including static constant folding of unreachable branches.
- Bit and range selects on the left-hand and right-hand sides of assignments.
- Multi-dimensional arrays and packed/unpacked struct member access.

SystemVerilog constructs that are not currently supported or have limited handling:

- Tasks and functions: calls are not followed; dependencies through function
  arguments are not tracked.
- Assertions and coverage constructs are ignored.
- System tasks and functions (@c $display, etc.) are ignored.
- @c force / @c release and @c deassign are not modelled.
- Dynamic types, classes, and constraint blocks are not modelled.
- Cross-module references (XMRs) are partially supported through slang's
  hierarchical reference resolution.


@section use-cases Potential use cases

Here are some ideas for ways in which RTL Wiring Inspector can be used in digital design.

- Connectivity checks. Testing for the existence of paths can be used to implement
  lightweight lint checks for design structure. For example, checking that a
  control signal fans out correctly to all memories in a design. Equally, the
  absence of paths can be also be checked, for example to assert that design
  components are correctly isolated.

- Static timing / critical-path estimation. By annotating edges with a simple
  unit-delay or gate-type-based delay model, the longest combinational path
  between any two register stages could be reported. Even without real timing
  analysis, logic depth is a useful early-stage proxy for critical paths and helps
  flag modules that will struggle to meet frequency targets.

- Clock domain crossing checks (CDC). This requires tracing connectivity across
  register boundaries and correlating with clock edges. Slang knows about
  EdgeKind but doesn't do cross-domain path analysis.

- Reset domain crossing checks (RDC). Tracing which registers share reset paths
  and flagging cross-domain reset issues. Similar to CDC but for resets.

- Cone-of-influence extraction. Given a target signal, extract the minimal
  sub-design (all transitive fan-in) needed to compute it. This could output a
  reduced SV module or a sub-graph DOT file, useful for debugging or feeding a
  smaller design into formal verification tools.

- Netlist diffing. Compare netlists from two revisions of the same design and
  report structural differences: added/removed signals, changed connectivity,
  altered fan-in/fan-out. This would be valuable to catch unintended RTL
  changes, as a lightweight netlist equivalence check.

- Hierarchical complexity metrics. Report per-module statistics such as node
  count, edge count, combinational depth, fan-out distribution, register count.
  These metrics could help identify overly complex modules that should be
  refactored and can track design complexity over time.

**/
